    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled by configuring `family inet6` under the interface unit.\\n2. Since the original Cisco config had `ipv6 enable` on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0 interfaces, both need to be translated.\\n3. Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (ge-x/y/z instead of GigabitEthernetx/y/z).\\n4. The `unit 0` is required in Juniper even for basic configurations.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, IPv6 is enabled by configuring `family inet6` under the interface unit.\\n2. Since the original Cisco config had `ipv6 enable` on both GigabitEthernet1/0/0 and GigabitEthernet2/0/0 interfaces, both need to be translated.\\n3. Juniper uses a different interface naming convention (ge-x/y/z instead of GigabitEthernetx/y/z).\\n4. The `unit 0` is required in Juniper even for basic configurations.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco `ipv6 address` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64\\n##\\n\\nThis command:\\n1. Specifies the interface (ge-2/0/0 equivalent to GigabitEthernet2/0/0)\\n2. Sets unit 0 (default in Juniper)\\n3. Configures IPv6 address family (inet6)\\n4. Assigns the same IPv6 address and prefix length (2001:db8:1::1/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper equivalent enables IPv6 on an interface by configuring the `inet6` family for the interface unit. Note that:\\n1. Juniper uses `ge` instead of `GigabitEthernet`\\n2. Juniper requires specifying a unit (typically 0) and the protocol family\\n3. I included both interfaces from the original config since `ipv6 enable` appeared on both', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper equivalent enables IPv6 on an interface by configuring the `inet6` family for the interface unit. Note that:\\n1. Juniper uses `ge` instead of `GigabitEthernet`\\n2. Juniper requires specifying a unit (typically 0) and the protocol family\\n3. I included both interfaces from the original config since `ipv6 enable` appeared on both', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
